{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"States of Matter","count":21,"questions":[{"id":51400,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"States of Matter","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"For one mole of a van der Waals' gas, the compressibility factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced></math> at a fixed volume will certainly decrease, if<br />\n[Given : \" a \" and \" b \" are standard parameters for van der Waals' gas]<br />\n ","options":["\" b \" increases and \" a \" decreases at constant temperature","\" b \" decreases and \"  a \" increases at constant temperature","temperature increases at constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></math> and \" b \" values<br />\n ","\"  b \" increases at constant \"  a \" and temperature"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> are used as notation of ideal pressure and ideal volume of a</p>\n\n<p>van der Waals' gas, the actual pressure and volume are:<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>b</mi></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><msup><mi>As</mi><mrow><mo>'</mo><mo>'</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>'</mo><mo>'</mo></mrow></msup></math>increases,  P decreases, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>80</mn><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></math><br />\ndecreases, when temperature is constant. and, as \" B ' decreases, V decreases, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>so</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></math><br />\ndecreases, when temperature is constant.<br />\nNote option (c) : When temperature increases, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mo> </mo><mi>V</mi></math> also increases and therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Z</mi></math> would not necessarily decrease.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SA)","_marksId":"614da48643154efc1ca12235"},{"id":51401,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"States of Matter","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"According to Graham's Law, the rate of diffusion of $\\mathrm{CO}, \\mathrm{O}_{2}, \\mathrm{~N}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ follows the order:","options":["$\\mathrm{CO}=\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\gt \\mathrm{O}_{2}\\gt \\mathrm{CO}_{2}$","$\\mathrm{CO}=\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\gt \\mathrm{CO}_{2}\\gt \\mathrm{O}_{2}$","$\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\gt \\mathrm{CO}=\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\gt \\mathrm{CO}_{2}$","$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}\\gt \\mathrm{O}_{2}\\gt \\mathrm{CO}=\\mathrm{N}_{2}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\mathrm{r} \\propto \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{M}}}$ Rate of diffusion decrease with increase in molecular weight Rate of diffusion order\n$\\mathrm{CO}=\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\gt \\mathrm{O}_{2}\\gt \\mathrm{CO}_{2}(28) \\quad(28) \\quad(32)\\quad(44)$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d213e7601f0d07921e21"},{"id":51402,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"States of Matter","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The volume vs. temperature graph of I mole of an ideal gas is given below. The pressure of the gas (in atm) at $X, Y$ and $Z$, respectively, are\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FXRSpmXfBbhtNMkOM6nSMt8aIR1atc7aYN1lPWyTcSb8.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FXRSpmXfBbhtNMkOM6nSMt8aIR1atc7aYN1lPWyTcSb8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FXRSpmXfBbhtNMkOM6nSMt8aIR1atc7aYN1lPWyTcSb8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["$0.328,0.820,0.820$","$3.28,8.20,3.28$","0.238, $0.280,0.280$","$32.8,0.280,82.0$"],"answer":0,"solution":"Using ideal gas equation, $\\mathrm{PV}=\\mathrm{nRT}$\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\mathrm{P}=\\frac{\\mathrm{nRT}}{\\mathrm{V}} ; \\quad \\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{x}}=\\frac{1 \\times 0.082 \\times 200}{50}=0.328 \\mathrm{~atm} \\\\\n\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{y}}=\\frac{1 \\times 0.082 \\times 500}{50}=0.820 \\mathrm{~atm} \\mathrm{P}_{x}=\\frac{1 \\times 0.082 \\times 200}{50}=0.820 \\mathrm{~atm}\n\\end{array}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213788d27d9a2d1b08f"},{"id":51403,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"States of Matter","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>A container of volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>24</mn><mo> </mo><mtext>L</mtext></math>can with stand a maximum pressure of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> atm at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>298</mn><mo> </mo><mtext>K</mtext></math> before exploding. The maximum amount of nitrogen (in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>g</mtext></math>) that can be safely put in this container at this temperature is closest to</p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo> </mo><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn></math>"],"answer":3,"solution":"<p>From ideal gas equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>p</mtext><mo> </mo><mtext>V</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>n</mtext><mo> </mo><mtext>R</mtext><mo> </mo><mtext>T</mtext></math></p>\n\n<p>maximum number of moles in container,</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>n</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>p</mtext><mtext>V</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0821</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>298</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>18</mn></math> moles Maximum weight of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mtext>N</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub></math> in container<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0183</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>28</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>127</mn><mo> </mo><mtext>g</mtext></math></p>\n\n<p>At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>127</mn><mo> </mo><mtext>g</mtext></math> exploding can occur. Thus, it must be less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>127</mn><mo> </mo></math> . Thus, the maximum amount of nitrogen that can be safely put in this container at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>298</mn></math> temperature and exert pressure less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo></math>atm will be closest to undefined.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SA)","_marksId":"614da3f70ff4347d941ede61"},{"id":51404,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"States of Matter","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"In a closed vessel, an ideal gas at $1 \\mathrm{~atm}$ is heated from $27^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ to $327^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$. the final pressure of the gas wil approximately be","options":["3 atm","$0.5 \\mathrm{~atm}$","2 atm","12 atm"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{P} \\propto \\mathrm{T}(\\mathrm{V}, \\mathrm{n} \\rightarrow$ const $)\\frac{\\mathrm{P}_{1}}{\\mathrm{P}_{2}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{T}_{1}}{\\mathrm{~T}_{2}}\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{P}_{2}}=\\frac{300}{600}\\mathrm{P}_{2}=2 \\mathrm{~atm}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d2134adf75ae1263c018"},{"id":51405,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"States of Matter","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The ratio of root mean square velocity of hydrogen at $50 \\mathrm{~K}$ to that of nitrogen at $500 \\mathrm{~K}$ is closest to","options":["$1.18$","$0.85$","$0.59$","$1.40$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{rms}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{3 \\mathrm{RT}}{\\mathrm{M}}}\\frac{\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{rms}}\\right)_{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}}{\\left(\\mathrm{~V}_{\\mathrm{rms}}\\right)_{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{\\frac{3 \\times \\mathrm{R} \\times 50}{2}}}{\\sqrt{\\frac{3 \\times \\mathrm{R} \\times 500}{28}}}=1.18$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213d2cd53dc4569f020"},{"id":51406,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"States of Matter","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The pressure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mfenced></math> and volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mfenced></math> isotherm of a van der Waals' gas, at the temperature at which it undergoes gas to liquid transition, is correctly represented by","options":["<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestion