20 \\mathrm{I}$. at $300 \\mathrm{~K}$. The amount of heat-change (in $\\mathrm{kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}$ ) in the process is $-$","options":["0","$7.2$","$10.2$","$17.2$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\mathrm{W}=-2.303 \\mathrm{n} \\mathrm{RT} \\log \\frac{\\mathrm{V}_{2}}{\\mathrm{~V}_{1}} \\\\\n=-2.303 \\times 3 \\times 8.314 \\quad 10^{-3} \\times 300 \\log \\frac{20}{2} \\\\\n=-17.2 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}\n\\end{array}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213d59d17933a7f71a2"},{"id":51414,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"States of Matter","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"At $298 \\mathrm{~K}$, assuming ideal behaviour, the average kinetic energy of a deuterium molecule is","options":["two times that of a hydrogen molecule","four times that of a hydrogen molecule","half of that of a hydrogen molecule","same as that of a hydrogen molecule"],"answer":3,"solution":"$(\\mathrm{K} . \\mathrm{E} .)_{\\text {average }}=\\frac{3}{2} \\mathrm{kT}$\ni.e., average kinetic energy depends only on temperature.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d213dbff7813428fdacb"},{"id":51415,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"States of Matter","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"As isolated box, equally partitioned contains two ideal gases $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{B}$ as shown\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F23KDoQtZK7ba9j7jsG4advZ3DMhNXxwshTpz4lBvBvc.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F23KDoQtZK7ba9j7jsG4advZ3DMhNXxwshTpz4lBvBvc.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F23KDoQtZK7ba9j7jsG4advZ3DMhNXxwshTpz4lBvBvc.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nWhen the partition is removed, the gases mix. The changes in enthalpy $(\\Delta \\mathrm{H})$ and entropy $(\\Delta \\mathrm{S})$ in the process, respectively, are","options":["zero, positive","zero, negative","positive, zero","negative, zero"],"answer":0,"solution":"According to KTG\nForce of attraction and repulsion amongst molecules of ideal gas are negligible \nSo, $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=0$\nand randomness increases due to increase in volume so $\\Delta \\mathrm{S}=+\\mathrm{ve}$.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d2137c11d6aa479faf00"},{"id":51416,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"States of Matter","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>The isotherms of a gas are shown below :</p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fb5b3109c-fc69-4bf4-8e26-4198e712e996-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fb5b3109c-fc69-4bf4-8e26-4198e712e996-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fb5b3109c-fc69-4bf4-8e26-4198e712e996-image.png?alt=media\"/></p>\n<p>Among the following,</p>\n<p>(i) At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>, the gas cannot be liquefied</p>\n<p>(ii) At point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>, liquid starts to appear at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p>\n<p>(ii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></msub></math> is the highest temperature at which the gas can be liquefied</p>\n<p>(iv) At point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>, a small increase in pressure condenses the whole system to a liquid</p>\n<p>The correct statements are :</p>","options":["only (i) and (ii)","only (i), (iii) and (iv)","only (ii), (iii) and (iv)","(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)"],"answer":3,"solution":"<p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></msub></math>, the gas cannot be liquefied at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></msub></math> is the highest temperature at which the gas can be liquefied. </p>\n\n<p>At temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>, liquid starts to appear at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>, however a small increase in pressure at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>  condenses the whole system to liquid. </p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SA)","_marksId":"614da4fc1ee8efe3c05d386e"},{"id":51417,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"States of Matter","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"In the following compressibility factor $(\\mathrm{Z})$ vs pressure graph at $300 \\mathrm{~K}$, the compressibility of $\\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at pressure $ \\lt  200$ bar deviates from ideal behaviour because<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FLtohaczhGIaQA4rvTd-QYdxGKyQlu2LBUO6JwJMzzAE.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FLtohaczhGIaQA4rvTd-QYdxGKyQlu2LBUO6JwJMzzAE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FLtohaczhGIaQA4rvTd-QYdxGKyQlu2LBUO6JwJMzzAE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["The molar volume of $\\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is less than its molar volume in the ideal state","The molar volume of $\\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is same as that in its ideal state","Intermolecular interactions between $\\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules decresases","The molar volume of $\\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is more than its molar volume in the ideal state"],"answer":0,"solution":"$Z=\\frac{\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{M}}\\right) \\mathrm{r}}{\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{M}}\\right)_{\\mathrm{i}}} \\lt  1$ at $\\mathrm{p} \\lt  200 \\mathrm{bar}\\therefore\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{M}}\\right)_{\\mathrm{r}} \\lt  \\left(\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{M}}\\right)_{\\mathrm{i}}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2131c8321be850554f5"},{"id":51418,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"States of Matter","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"At a constant pressure $P$, the plot of volume $(V)$ as a function of temperature $(T)$ for 2 moles of an ideal gas gives a straight line with a slope $0.328 \\mathrm{~L} \\mathrm{~K}^{-1}$. The value of $\\mathrm{P}$ (in atm) is closest to [Gas constant, $\\mathrm{R}=0.0821 \\mathrm{~L} \\mathrm{~atm} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\mathrm{~K}^{-1}$ ]","options":["$0.25$","$0.5$","$1.0$","$2.0$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$P V=n R T\\Rightarrow \\frac{V}{T}=\\frac{n R}{P}=$ slope\n$\\Rightarrow P=\\frac{n R}{\\text { slope }}=\\frac{2 \\ti